# ChecklistDek Compliance

Gate before presenting final code. If any checklist item is "no", fix it first.

See `checklistdek-project.mdc` (architecture), `checklistdek-standards.mdc` (quality), `wordpress-php.mdc` (PHP/REST patterns).

## Security

- Sanitize on input, escape on output, authorize before action.
- REST: every route has `permission_callback`; use `CHECKLISTDEK_Capabilities::current_user_can()`.
- Admin UI auth: `X-WP-Nonce` with `wp_rest` nonce on all REST `fetch` calls and admin-ajax (`postAjax()` sends POST `nonce` + header); nonce refreshes via Heartbeat `rest_nonce` on hub and client panel scripts. `CHECKLISTDEK_Admin_Ajax::verify_ajax_nonce()` reads POST or `HTTP_X_WP_NONCE` (`get_request_nonce()`); invalid nonce returns JSON error 403, not `-1`.
- Never expose decrypted Application Passwords or WP Umbrella Public API tokens in REST responses, logs, or audit entries.
- Store credentials via `CHECKLISTDEK_Credential_Vault::encrypt()`; decrypt only at request time in `CHECKLISTDEK_Remote_Client` (site app passwords) or WP Umbrella adapter / `CHECKLISTDEK_Settings::get_wp_umbrella_api_token()` (hub connector outbound only).
- Settings API: `sanitize_callback`, `show_in_rest => false`. When adding settings keys updated outside the Settings form (REST, admin-ajax, direct `update_option`), extend `CHECKLISTDEK_Settings::sanitize()` so **Save Changes** on Settings does not strip them (`onboarding_dismissed`, `wp_umbrella_api_token_enc`, `custom_site_groups`). Encrypted values (`wp_umbrella_api_token_enc`) must not pass through `sanitize_text_field` — preserve the vault ciphertext (null-byte strip only).
- Remote requests: validate URLs with `esc_url_raw()`; use `wp_remote_*` with timeouts; handle `WP_Error`. Strip HTML from remote/transport error text via `CHECKLISTDEK_Remote_Client::sanitize_remote_message()` before connection event logs, `last_error`, or REST/admin-facing messages (`request()`, `test_connection()`, `log_connection()`).
- Admin JS notices: use `notice()` for plain text (HTML-escaped) or `noticeHtml()` with `escHtml()` / `escAttr()` for trusted inline links — do not inject unescaped API or remote error strings into notice HTML.
- SQL: `$wpdb->prepare()` only; no user input in table names. Plugin-owned tables: resolve names via repository `::table()` / `::steps_table()` / `CHECKLISTDEK_Audit_Log::table_name()` and pass with the `%i` identifier placeholder (WP 6.2+). For dynamic `IN (...)` lists, use `%d`/`%s` placeholders only — table names must never come from request input.
- Webhook URLs: sanitize with `esc_url_raw()`; only POST on configured user events.
- Email notification addresses: sanitize via `CHECKLISTDEK_Settings::sanitize_email_notification_addresses()` (`sanitize_email` per comma-separated entry); recipients resolved by `get_email_notification_addresses()` (falls back to site admin email when unset).

## Remote Site Access

- Core automation model: WordPress core REST API + Application Passwords for API checklist steps, connection tests, and drift reads.
- Client checklist panel (bundled mu-plugin): required on each managed site for checklist UI in client wp-admin. **Full/Pro hub** (`checklistdek_can_auto_deploy_client_panel()`): auto-deploys to `wp-content/mu-plugins/` when a site is saved or tested with credentials, on initial checklist push, or via manual **Refresh Client Panel** — user-initiated, not undisclosed; `POST /client/install` exists only when `class-rest-api-install.php` is in the client bundle. **WordPress.org Community hub**: no automatic writes to remote `mu-plugins/` or hub uploads for installer ZIPs — manual bootstrap download + upload + **Retry panel install** (verify only). When the panel is missing, admin UI surfaces **Client checklist panel setup**. Panel files are whitelisted bundle copies only; install route validates paths and requires hub Application Password auth. Routine step toggles and hub `sync_run()` do not reinstall the bundle (`push_run( $id, false, false )`).
- Connection tests must not leak credentials in error messages shown to non-privileged users.
- Client checklist panel UI and client REST routes (`can_view_panel`, `CHECKLISTDEK_Client_Run_Store::user_can_view_panel()`) require `read`; `manage_options` always sees the run; with a valid step list, the user must match at least one step `target_roles` (empty `target_roles` = any logged-in user with `read`, same as step completion); empty/malformed snapshots require `edit_posts`; panel assets and render hooks must not load for users who fail this check.
- Checklist JSON import (`POST /checklists/import`, `CHECKLISTDEK_Checklist_Repository::import()`) must not honor client-supplied `is_template` / `is_vault` flags (always stored as custom checklists).
- Payload mapper must whitelist/validate endpoint paths and methods before remote execution. Mutating `/wp/v2/settings` payloads must pass through `CHECKLISTDEK_Payload_Mapper::filter_excluded_payload()` so hub `exclude_options` strip sensitive fields before remote execution; audit `api_step_executed` may include `excluded_fields` when fields were stripped.
- Checklist `deep_link` values are wp-admin paths — store via `CHECKLISTDEK_Admin_Deep_Links::normalize_stored_path()`, not `esc_url_raw()` (which strips bare paths like `options-general.php`). Full URLs are built at display/push time via `CHECKLISTDEK_Remote_Client::admin_link()` or client `admin_url()`.
- Drift verification reads remote state for comparison only — no arbitrary code execution.
- Client run callbacks use per-run `client_run_token` (`hash_equals` in `can_client_token_access_run`; `ensure_client_token()` backfills legacy runs) — never expose tokens in hub REST responses, logs, or audit entries shown to non-privileged users; token travels only in hub→client run snapshots and client→hub callback headers.
- Client step notes require non-empty text; save locally on the remote site first, then sync to hub. Hub note storage uses `filter_step_notes()` to drop empty/duplicate entries. Optional screenshot attachments are gated by per-step `show_screenshot_field` (requires `show_note_field`); use existing remote media library attachment IDs only (`wp.media` on the client site) — not arbitrary file uploads to the hub.
- Client panel **Dismiss** clears the local completed-run snapshot on the remote site only; it does not delete hub run records.
- Auto-capture is user-initiated (`can_edit_checklists` on hub, logged-in client admin on remote); records only whitelisted admin actions (REST settings mutations, plugin activate/deactivate) into ephemeral client option data — not background telemetry.
- Notifications: Community builds use a no-op webhook dispatcher stub; full builds `CHECKLISTDEK_Webhook_Dispatcher::dispatch( $event, $data )` returns immediately when `!CHECKLISTDEK_Licensing::can_use_notifications()`; otherwise calls `CHECKLISTDEK_Email_Notifier::dispatch()` first (wp_mail when event is in `email_notification_events`; recipients via `get_email_notification_addresses()`), then Slack/Discord/Teams webhooks only when the event is listed in `notification_events` and a channel URL is configured (empty `notification_events` skips all webhook POSTs). Drift alerts are user-opt-in via `drift_detected` and only dispatch when new drift checks appear after verification—not as undisclosed recurring telemetry.
- Legacy audit actions (`workflow_created`, `workflow_updated`) and webhook `details.workflow` are read-only backward-compat shims — new code must use `checklist_*` keys and `details.checklist`.

## Directory & Privacy

- No obfuscated code or undisclosed telemetry.
- Outbound requests are limited to: user-registered remote sites (including mu-plugin deploy/push on Pro hubs only — Community hub does not push installer files), user-configured webhook URLs, WordPress `wp_mail()` for opt-in email notifications (no third-party mail API unless the site configures SMTP), and integration adapters (user-initiated site sync; client panel push via MainWP Connect only on Pro hubs when Application Passwords are not configured; WP Umbrella uses the user-configured Public API token at `public-api.wp-umbrella.com`, including a lightweight verify probe on token save). Document third-party connectors in `readme.txt` **External services**. **Freemius SDK** (full/premium distribution only — not loaded or shipped in the Community / WordPress.org zip) may contact Freemius for account, licensing, and updates per the SDK; keep `is_org_compliant` and omit `checklistdek-freemius.php` + `vendor/` from Community builds (`bin/build-community-zip.php`).
- Integrations **Telemetry Sync Mapping Rules** store field-mapping preferences only (`telemetry_sync_rules`); they do not emit tracking data.
- Webhook and integration behavior must be documented in `readme.txt`. Email notification behavior (opt-in events, `wp_mail`, recipient fallback) must also be documented in `readme.txt`.
- No spammy readme or deceptive behavior.

## Code Quality

- Prefer WordPress core APIs over custom hacks.
- Keep code human-readable for directory review.
- Follow WPCS across PHP, CSS, JS, and HTML.
- Enqueue assets via `is_plugin_screen()` only.
- Use `checklistdek` / `CHECKLISTDEK_` prefix consistently.

## Self-Review Checklist

- [ ] No WordPress core modifications
- [ ] GPL-compatible
- [ ] Human-readable, reviewable code
- [ ] User-facing strings translatable (text domain `checklistdek`)
- [ ] REST routes: capability checks on every endpoint
- [ ] Credentials encrypted at rest when enabled; never leaked in API responses
- [ ] Input sanitized; output escaped
- [ ] SQL prepared in repositories (and `CHECKLISTDEK_Audit_Log`); plugin tables via `%i` where supported (`::table()` / `::table_name()` helpers)
- [ ] Remote calls use timeouts and error handling
- [ ] Webhooks and email notifications opt-in and user-configured
- [ ] Uninstall via `CHECKLISTDEK_Uninstaller::run()` drops all tables, plugin options, and capabilities (Community `register_uninstall_hook`; full Freemius `after_uninstall`)
- [ ] Unique `CHECKLISTDEK_` / `checklistdek_` prefix
- [ ] Community zip excludes Freemius bootstrap, SDK (`vendor/`), and Pro-only PHP/templates
- [ ] Would survive WordPress.org review

## Unsafe Requests

If a feature conflicts with the Plugin Handbook, Coding Standards, security best practices, privacy expectations, or Directory Guidelines — refuse the unsafe approach and propose a compliant alternative.
